paperweight-skel (0.2.71-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * greetd login screen: fix wallpaper appearing zoomed in/cropped, hiding
    the branded wordmark. gtkgreet's -b/--background flag (added in
    0.2.70-1) is documented by gtkgreet-1(1) itself as anchoring the image
    to the upper-left corner at 1:1 and not handling DPI scaling values
    other than 1 correctly — on any scaled display this crops into the
    image's corner. Switched to embedding the wallpaper as a
    "window { background-image: ...; background-size: cover; }" rule
    appended onto /etc/greetd/gtkgreet.css by paperweight-set-greeter-theme
    (the same approach already used for gtklock), which scales correctly
    at any DPI. start-greeter no longer passes -b; /etc/greetd/background
    is now written for diagnostics only.

 -- Alex Long <johnalexanderlong@gmail.com>  Sun, 12 Jul 2026 17:27:45 -0500

paperweight-skel (0.2.70-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * Waybar launcher: replace the generic Nerd Font glyph with an actual
    PaperweightOS logo (rock.png, the same asset fastfetch uses) via
    waybar's "image" module. The "path" key does not expand $HOME or
    "~" (it's opened directly, not through a shell), so the module uses
    "exec": "echo $HOME/.config/waybar/paperweight-logo.png" instead,
    which does run through a shell. CSS selector for a "type#name"
    module (as opposed to "custom/name", which becomes "#custom-name")
    turned out to be "#launcher" with GTK also matching "#image.launcher"
    and "image#launcher" — the stylesheet targets all three since the
    exact selector waybar assigns isn't documented. Icon size 20px (not
    24px) to stay within the existing 40px bar height; 24px pushed
    waybar to request 42px.
  * greetd login screen: pass gtkgreet's native -b/--background flag
    with the active theme's branded wallpaper
    (/usr/share/paperweight-os/wallpapers/<theme>-wallpaper2.jpg,
    falling back to -wallpaper1.jpg then the flat <theme>.png if
    paperweight-wallpapers isn't installed). start-greeter reads the
    path from the new /etc/greetd/background file; paperweight-set-
    greeter-theme writes it alongside the existing gtkgreet.css copy
    so it stays in sync with theme switches. Verified standalone that
    -b composites behind the existing gtkgreet.css (which only styles
    the login card, not the full window) without conflict.
  * gtklock lock screen: add background-image (theme's branded
    wallpaper, same selection convention as greetd above) to each
    theme's window rule, with background-color kept as a fallback.
    gtklock links libgtk-3, not GTK4, so the "invalid property rejects
    the whole stylesheet" GTK4 gotcha (see this file's Known Issues)
    does not apply here.

 -- Alex Long <johnalexanderlong@gmail.com>  Sun, 12 Jul 2026 00:00:00 -0500

paperweight-skel (0.2.69-1) trixie; urgency=low

  * Give fastfetch a real logo: source it from paperweight-plymouth's
    rock.png — the rock alone, without the paper stack, since fine detail
    doesn't survive downsampling to character art — and render it via
    fastfetch's "chafa" logo type (ANSI truecolor character blocks),
    replacing the built-in "small" Debian ASCII placeholder. Chose
    "chafa" over "auto": observed "auto" dump raw PNG bytes when a
    terminal doesn't answer fastfetch's capability query, where "chafa"
    falls back cleanly to the built-in Debian logo on the same failure;
    "chafa" also renders as plain ANSI text, which — unlike sixel/kitty
    graphics — works inside tmux without passthrough configuration.
  * Set both logo width and height explicitly instead of width alone
    with preserveAspectRatio: leaving height implicit let fastfetch
    miscompute the logo's row count, desyncing the cursor math used to
    align info lines next to it (fragments of the logo bled onto the end
    of some info lines; others lost their left indent).
  * Render the logo with "chafa": { "fgOnly": true }: chafa draws only
    foreground-colored glyphs and leaves every cell's background
    untouched, so the terminal's own translucent, wallpaper-blended
    background shows through around the rock instead of a drawn-in
    color. (Tried pre-compositing the source onto a flat per-theme
    background color first, to fix chafa defaulting transparent edge
    pixels to black — fastfetch's chafa integration has no way to tell
    it what to blend against. That produced an opaque patch that didn't
    match the terminal's real background, which looked worse than the
    fringe it replaced; fgOnly avoids both problems and keeps the logo
    theme-agnostic — one shared PNG again, no Pillow dependency needed.)
  * Widen the key column (8 -> 10 chars): "Battery", the longest key
    label, was exactly one column short of leaving room for the
    key/value separator, so the percentage ran directly into it with no
    space (e.g. "Battery69%").
  * Rebrand /etc/os-release as "PaperweightOS 0.1" (PRETTY_NAME/NAME/
    VERSION/VERSION_ID/HOME_URL/SUPPORT_URL/BUG_REPORT_URL, plus
    ID_LIKE=debian) via an idempotent postinst sed patch, since
    /etc/os-release is owned by the Essential base-files package and
    can't be shipped directly. ID stays "debian" deliberately, to avoid
    breaking tooling that keys off an exact ID match. This is what makes
    fastfetch's OS line show "PaperweightOS 0.1" instead of "Debian GNU/
    Linux 13 (trixie)" — no fastfetch-specific override needed. Distro
    version is a new, separate major.minor number bumped at milestones,
    independent of individual .deb package versions (see CLAUDE.md).

 -- Alex Long <johnalexanderlong@gmail.com>  Sun, 12 Jul 2026 00:00:00 -0500

